1
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:01,000
The incredible story of the team that changed the game forever.

2
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:56,000
Glory Road is a drama, history film directed by James Gartner.

3
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:51,000
In 1966, Texas Western coach Don Haskins led the first all-black starting line-up
for a college basketball team to the NCAA national championship.

4
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:46,000
Don Haskins, played by Josh Lucas.

5
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:41,000
Bobby Joe Hill, played by Derek Luke.

6
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:36,000
Adolph Rupp, played by Jon Voight.

7
00:34:25,000 --> 00:34:31,000
Jerry Armstrong, played by Austin Nichols.

8
00:39:20,000 --> 00:39:26,000
Moe Iba, played by Evan Jones.

9
00:44:15,000 --> 00:44:21,000
Harry Flournoy, played by Mehcad Brooks.

10
00:49:10,000 --> 00:49:16,000
Glory Road is a 2006 American sports drama film directed by James Gartner, based on a true story
surrounding the events leading to the 1966 NCAA University Division Basketball Championship.

11
00:54:05,000 --> 00:54:11,000
Don Haskins portrayed by Josh Lucas, head coach of Texas Western College (now known as University of Texas at
El Paso or UTEP), coached a team with an all-black starting lineup, a first in NCAA history.

12
00:59:00,000 --> 00:59:06,000
Glory Road explores racism, discrimination and student athletics.

13
01:03:55,000 --> 01:04:01,000
Supporting actors Derek Luke and Jon Voight also star in principal roles.

14
01:08:50,000 --> 01:08:56,000
The film was a co-production between the motion picture studios of Walt Disney Pictures,
Jerry Bruckheimer Films, Texas Western Productions, and Glory Road Productions.

15
01:13:45,000 --> 01:13:51,000
It was commercially distributed by Buena Vista Pictures theatrically and by the
Buena Vista Home Entertainment division for the video rental market.

16
01:18:40,000 --> 01:18:46,000
It premiered in theaters nationwide in the United States on January 13, 2006, grossing
$42,938,449 in box office business despite generally mixed reviews from critics.

17
01:23:35,000 --> 01:23:41,000
Glory Road was nominated for a number of awards including the Humanitas
Prize; the film won the 2006 ESPY Award for Best Sports Movie.

18
01:28:30,000 --> 01:28:36,000
On January 10, 2006, the original motion picture soundtrack was
released by the Hollywood Records music label.

19
01:33:25,000 --> 01:33:31,000
The soundtrack was composed and orchestrated by musician Trevor Rabin.

20
01:38:20,000 --> 01:38:26,000
The DVD release, featuring theatrical trailers, extended interviews with players and colleagues of
coach Haskins, and deleted scenes, among other highlights, was released in the U.S.

21
01:43:15,000 --> 01:43:21,000
on June 6, 2006.

22
01:48:10,000 --> 01:48:16,000
The film explores themes of racial segregation, based on
true story, basketball, teachers and students.

23
01:53:05,000 --> 01:53:11,000
The tone is emotional.
